{"doc_id": "doc5636", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "A new design for ten-shilling notes was introduced in 1961, with the old notes ceasing to be legal tender in 1962. These new series C notes were slightly longer and narrower, and were the first ten-shilling notes to feature a portrait of Queen Elizabeth II on the front. The reverse design incorporated the logo of the Bank of England.[1] In the late 1960s it was decided that future banknotes should feature a British historical figure on the reverse. The first such note was the series D £20 note, first issued in 1970, featuring William Shakespeare. A design for a ten-shilling note featuring Walter Raleigh on the reverse was approved in 1964 but was never issued.[3] Instead, in 1969, as part of the process of decimalisation, a new fifty pence coin was introduced as a replacement for the ten-shilling note. The principal reason for the change was economy: the notes had an average lifetime of about five months whereas coins could last at least fifty years.[4] The series C ten shilling notes ceased to be legal tender on 22 November 1970.[1]", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 2058766745, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}, {"attempt": 2, "generated_text": "A new design for ten-shilling notes was introduced in 1961, with the old notes ceasing to be legal tender in 1962. These new series C notes were slightly longer and narrower, and were the first ten-shilling notes to feature a portrait of Queen Elizabeth II on the front. The reverse design incorporated the logo of the Bank of England. In the late 1960s it was decided that future banknotes should feature a British historical figure on the reverse, and the series D £20 note, first issued in 1970, featuring William Shakespeare, was the first such note. A design for a ten-shilling note featuring Walter Raleigh on the reverse was approved in 1964 but was never issued. Instead, in 1969, as part of the process of decimalisation, a new fifty pence coin was introduced as a replacement for the ten-shilling note. The principal reason for the change was economy: the notes had an average lifetime of about five months whereas coins could last at least fifty years. The series C ten shilling notes ceased to be legal tender on 22 November 1970.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 2003043596, "similarity": 0.9679579148732664, "temperature": 0.5}], "fingerprint": "78ba763936425122376c73b27ccebe9358f9d9565217df843bebd2d3987565ff", "generation_attempts": 3, "gold_answer": "22 November 1970", "matched_aliases": ["22 November 1970", "1970"], "query_id": "test153", "question_hash": "b0ace4aac0ad37fe3dd0980647837cf0a3d4f6743d5dd7e514f9c40fe55309c3", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "585fcf43fa8308fae20285838a2e3131b8788e438f2acffe0f4de2aa6cd879df", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 520848583, "similarity": 0.6830188679245283, "source_content_hash": "d666496ecdd7a0baf960f584136bc05848ef429d512e19c3c7b31239659a8c17", "source_hash": "fe0c0926eadad30a86c932907df911070f7271e500ee436fba554265a6d3b5c5", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.7, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "218136503d79f418147db8935498025f788be86aaa03985ca0b84937835a9660", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc5638", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "The Golden State Warriors are an American professional basketball team based in the San Francisco Bay Area in Oakland, California. The Warriors compete in the National Basketball Association (NBA) as a member of the league's Western Conference Pacific Division. The Warriors play their home games at the Oracle Arena in Oakland. The Warriors have reached nine NBA Finals, winning five NBA championships in 1947, 1956, 1975, 2015 and 2017. Golden State's five NBA championships are tied for fourth-most in NBA history with the San Antonio Spurs, and behind only the Boston Celtics (17), Los Angeles Lakers (16) and Chicago Bulls (6).", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1871559546, "similarity": 0.9976322020520916, "temperature": 0.3}, {"attempt": 2, "generated_text": "The Golden State Warriors are an American professional basketball team based in the San Francisco Bay Area in Oakland, California. The Warriors compete in the National Basketball Association (NBA) as a member of the league's Western Conference Pacific Division. The Warriors play their home games at the Oracle Arena in Oakland. The Warriors have reached nine NBA Finals, winning five NBA championships in 1947, 1956, 1975, 2015 and 2017. Golden State's five NBA championships are tied for fourth-most in NBA history with the San Antonio Spurs, and behind only the Boston Celtics (17), Los Angeles Lakers (16) and Chicago Bulls (6).", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1659151563, "similarity": 0.9976322020520916, "temperature": 0.5}], "fingerprint": "a04841a7b79bf79c004456fb756a47d5057c1c1334e4644d6c434d76d46cf0c6", "generation_attempts": 3, "gold_answer": "1947", "matched_aliases": ["1947"], "query_id": "test154", "question_hash": "43f8e2ca28af837c49d4ba3ab27f2388673f4d20e222756e9af874677a4b57b6", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "02db410451c8ed57a2a7adba626626d763b47e172d792df1cf5f00966244b8f9", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 625666347, "similarity": 0.4937799043062201, "source_content_hash": "9c91aa36479a2182700c8fdd6f6818d2cf1ed50b1efce6cc751d353160fe998d", "source_hash": "bcbea7b7a9fbca9b3accd212e99f431fd84c03f205e741e9b5202615fe4af07e", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.7, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "6a7c03d40b0a923e513d625ee4d6c7cb7f4fbe5a4d946ac81e07243b4d0d8a40", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc5758", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "During the first half of the twentieth century, John B. Watson devised methodological behaviorism, which rejected introspective methods and sought to understand behavior by only measuring observable behaviors and events. It was not until the 1930s that B. F. Skinner suggested that private events—including thoughts and feelings—should be subjected to the same controlling variables as observable behavior, which became the basis for his philosophy called radical behaviorism.[1][2] While Watson and Ivan Pavlov investigated the stimulus-response procedures of classical conditioning, Skinner assessed the controlling nature of consequences and also its' potential effect on the antecedents (or discriminative stimuli) that strengthens behavior; the technique became known as operant conditioning.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 2019928928, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "f1e205a5a14f015f34bf37abd64d188bd405d22ee6c44fd065a840320df1cf83", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "John B. Watson", "matched_aliases": ["John B. Watson"], "query_id": "test156", "question_hash": "d2600da4049ed77e536f87ca85bd6540d98f46516445b55b8baec338e7e84415", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "cc7d257c80fe9a12e6df3b49dcf9f80ea087ef62a6f8e6f0bb2653a15f9ce228", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 475632920, "similarity": 0.17633760361718162, "source_content_hash": "284483191751ddfce392c4f478cf22d04a3da2d3a9858efdabfeacb2a118f326", "source_hash": "198762127cbd502b729820e5bdc660be246901dbc14eb9abfd864774c325a6f7", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "4dcbe07dc9605c2814225fdd5cae9d101a0f247f4ef8c59934c3cfe91c6d33e8", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc5794", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Since at least the mid-19th century, numerous concepts for a union among Canada, Mexico and the United States of America, some including the Caribbean, the Central American and the South American countries, have been proposed, such as the North American Technate. Following the North American Free Trade Agreement (NAFTA) and the Maastricht Treaty going into effect there was speculation about the formation of a North American Union, similar to the European Union created by Maastricht, being a possible future step for the region.[12][13][14] Several proposals for continental integration of North America advocated the creation of a union styled after the European Union, though many academic and business groups advocated less dramatic changes involving the formation of a customs union or common market.[15][16]", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1118920009, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "f500438185d80822cf622d90044b2a441ba7645c61c0ad31aaff53c7c8e09444", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Canada", "matched_aliases": ["Canada"], "query_id": "test157", "question_hash": "75b2fa43d5571af6aa5580b45ebf962d011a2606fadd31626fcfcf50d2903ecf", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "826eda2858aec9c209360312a6a45bd6f72e69bc71e2a76ab901207ad302dd66", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1922267609, "similarity": 0.5190039318479686, "source_content_hash": "543299ff7fc8b7664059fb8c6c3791bfb83472111e49e3330b7bbcd739330cf2", "source_hash": "f370c451f72cf3bb9870833d3dea7feed2c9ecca0d25d5f42054f8277104457c", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "9042e82f595cfc8684dc496991a4cde3c0dd607ba45153702effced29d61c8b2", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc5819", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "The Ranjit Sagar Dam, also known as the Thein Dam, is part of a hydroelectric project constructed by the Government of Punjab on the Ravi River on the Border of two states of India Jammu and Kashmir and Punjab. 60% of the lake is part of J&K. The project is situated near Pathankot city in Pathankot district of the state of Punjab and Kathua city and Basholi tehsil of Kathua district in J&K. The project is the largest hydroelectric dam of the state of Punjab. The township where the site is located is called Shah pur Kandi Township. Feasibility studies for the project began in 1953 and geotechnical studies continued until 1980. Construction began in 1981, the generators were commissioned in 2000 and the project complete in March 2001.[4]", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 19701521, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "416375a0d690783fa36388ec276d85c46527e4765d4e665f7276a2f2e249063f", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Ravi River", "matched_aliases": ["Ravi River"], "query_id": "test158", "question_hash": "4aeeee693e3cbe71f9eaa4365e22b0fe6f09cee86241054385cee97408bedf90", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "57a78438ba77e916a840f0f8c3ca73217738003e5c0f4789e7c5e06d4534a809", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 226113925, "similarity": 0.429075511644319, "source_content_hash": "92cc60c87752a7849a5919410876333138d3c22cf610b41b5a7cfcfc12edba7d", "source_hash": "9a60e55eb3a247165c62ad7cd39835f6322f153c2b08b2682145098d3bee111f", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "edb6efa4902db55bd4edf944afad2627eda517107bf09aae971a3e70d44df938", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc5822", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Valinor (Land of the Valar) is a fictional location in J. R. R. Tolkien's legendarium, the realm of the Valar in Aman. It was also known as the Undying Lands, along with Tol Eressëa and the outliers of Aman. This latter name is somewhat misleading; the land itself, while blessed, did not cause mortals to live forever. However, only immortal beings were generally allowed to reside there. Exceptionally, the surviving bearers of the One Ring were allowed to dwell there for a time--Bilbo and Frodo Baggins and Sam Gamgee--and perhaps[dubious – discuss] Gimli son of Glóin who, it is said, accompanied his friend Legolas to Valinor.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1334242874, "similarity": 0.992930086410055, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "94fc3e18ea17c390f68d44ecffe98cc604dbce2d22273ec1296c47fe8ff650c9", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Valinor", "matched_aliases": ["Valinor"], "query_id": "test159", "question_hash": "b9663fae0b395659ba392f8771e43afc17a9dfac2ba7422d267670c087ba43ec", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "9c796a0f67853406d83ed02b3e50b52f2e4c8476f2368f363b8611f02d80d1c5", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 991409130, "similarity": 0.0336417157275021, "source_content_hash": "7a48793dad765aed5b4a18e679243032f9bf9e7f310df0e1a800d623829b5650", "source_hash": "c66b4f7dd6b0953dc5c6c558ad342ed8f76daf5219b64e27e516b31533829b13", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "520db42f38a7bf7bfb3be59d31223c5e165101043634f5c7666b452241d17da4", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc5831", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": false, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "After the destruction of Númenor, the Undying Lands were removed from Arda so that Men could not reach them. The Elves could go there only by the Straight Road and in ships capable of passing out of the spheres of the earth.", "reason": "missing_gold_answer", "seed": 1089409433, "similarity": null, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "f6c8a4597541ce82ee81f296fb55350071eb2e40559516dccadd47a0bc3e2407", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Valinor", "matched_aliases": ["Valinor"], "query_id": "test159", "question_hash": "b9663fae0b395659ba392f8771e43afc17a9dfac2ba7422d267670c087ba43ec", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "4a3f02ffcfb1fdf6642bcc0c64353e4217dbfdfe828d92220cf3f206d5d47f89", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 25400334, "similarity": 0.7845884413309983, "source_content_hash": "680ce65ded29671c455255cfa8afcf0cc369a1dc499ec7349052b1277ae8a37e", "source_hash": "b44343fd9fbbd6e328ff1f012c057731b5a96aa90cdcf544580dc6547365fd0f", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "3895e5a5373d0b3393a203f67e2880f4021e2e976fde531b9ebc78b244202ad2", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc5875", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Felix Baumgartner (German: [ˈfeːlɪks ˈbaʊ̯mˌɡaɐ̯tnɐ]; born 20 April 1969) is an Austrian skydiver, daredevil, and BASE jumper. He is best known for jumping to Earth from a helium balloon in the stratosphere on 14 October 2012. Doing so, he set world records for skydiving an estimated 39 km (24 mi), reaching an estimated top speed of 1,357.64 km/h (843.6 mph), or Mach 1.25. He became the first person to break the sound barrier without vehicular power relative to the surface on his descent. He broke skydiving records for exit altitude, vertical freefall distance without drogue, and vertical speed without drogue. Though he still holds the two latter records, the first was broken two years later, when on 24 October 2014, Alan Eustace jumped from 135,890 feet – or, 41.42 km (25.74 mi) with a drogue.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 41836709, "similarity": 0.9749847467968273, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "3d4a51bd03054bfedabf89f845d3e1fc79252a73ec94c4738821b34f6ea1e349", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Felix Baumgartner", "matched_aliases": ["Felix Baumgartner"], "query_id": "test161", "question_hash": "3e337080bb127ad84f0c3566e69a178a242c21a4f6214434be0b3539f1174b75", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "9b8a31f3b85953fc6553c3b2b02272820ac9dcceafc935ec03bdfa80970175d0", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1799335031, "similarity": 0.3256965944272446, "source_content_hash": "bc3912b72a5ccb9e97b8df6320df29d73cfa06f29ac7dea13aff3503f0f85168", "source_hash": "671b169ad9a5b2495f2ba384833f900688d774faa49693ffaaf047c26c57665c", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "c388c10f141cfa4dd4ce93f704849e8395bacd35d47d673d0772f5c8c377305d", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
