{"doc_id": "doc8930", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "RMS Titanic (/taɪˈtænɪk/) was a British passenger liner that sank in the North Atlantic Ocean in the early morning hours of 15 April 1912, after it collided with an iceberg during its maiden voyage from Southampton to New York City. There were an estimated 2,224 passengers and crew aboard the ship, and more than 1,500 died, making it one of the deadliest commercial peacetime maritime disasters in modern history. The RMS Titanic was the largest ship afloat at the time it entered service and was the second of three Olympic-class ocean liners operated by the White Star Line. The Titanic was built by the Harland and Wolff shipyard in Belfast. Thomas Andrews, her architect, died in the disaster.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 354873971, "similarity": 0.9964311206281228, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "cc0aee13eaf557916d2d7da550e49af2714cc9f4784533da0cf51a00e6ca3a54", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "North Atlantic Ocean", "matched_aliases": ["North Atlantic Ocean"], "query_id": "test243", "question_hash": "b47b32b2868c30421d9b1bc3b08feb6395b8007b49b35027e595fb8daa8e5fd6", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "db19cdd539d920a7937d332b5e64c427535ac7ced81cb1827ba8bf467fcf9098", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1643803672, "similarity": 0.4586080586080586, "source_content_hash": "a1136250d67afd6e088edb63e930754dd10d3821a5602ae6683ccd9b699b9462", "source_hash": "e193891877b790ea4a3f5c3ddba098507d6ff676291dcd4afd800b7d9308ada5", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "b351eedb52644be51b4a6e01c24495b0e4422d3bda4dbca3b6fc96f8f0126390", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc9074", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "The Lincoln Highway was one of the earliest transcontinental highways for automobiles across the United States of America. Conceived in 1912 by Indiana entrepreneur Carl G. Fisher, and formally dedicated October 31, 1913, the Lincoln Highway ran coast-to-coast from Times Square in New York City west to Lincoln Park in San Francisco, originally through 13 states: New York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Ohio, Indiana, Illinois, Iowa, Nebraska, Colorado, Wyoming, Utah, Nevada, and California. In 1915, the \"Colorado Loop\" was removed, and in 1928, a realignment relocated the Lincoln Highway through the northern tip of West Virginia. Thus, there are a total of 14 states, 128 counties, and more than 700 cities, towns and villages through which the highway passed at some time in its history.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 142801568, "similarity": 0.9981072555205047, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "679f51c40bb9e39f0e56153ecea52b3270920145f6a3c5b99ea3b86b7e2a6f2c", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Lincoln Park in San Francisco", "matched_aliases": ["Lincoln Park in San Francisco"], "query_id": "test246", "question_hash": "c555edc9279dc79cb26a6b3b566c7cec4f54f3cb94912a8c7b923bc565c36cee", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "85f35fc728645ab5dc58c610d14c951a7116d409986c543aa8069e32c4526f40", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1914382842, "similarity": 0.8155216284987278, "source_content_hash": "21f130086bf62050a0511877a337cd656b62070a5059d12e6b318f940dca5756", "source_hash": "f2d5588d0d7de54287e0ca4c2eb366122bfaaf0f85e948ecdfe6a7e2ae18e395", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "08c205d4d4f163e7108b8f3d2bd3f38ce27cbc912b38cd2c0359e3a8180475be", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc9184", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Historians debating the origins of the American Civil War focus on the reasons why seven Southern states declared their secession from the United States (the Union), why they united to form the Confederate States of America (or simply known as the \"Confederacy\"), and why the North refused to let them go. While most historians agree that conflicts over slavery caused the war, they disagree sharply regarding which kinds of conflict—ideological, economic, political, or social—were most important.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 800416264, "similarity": 0.996996996996997, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "46b119e0efea0aa88cc8b56fbfa01df9388749bfadc2eff47c47bab28faa331f", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "slavery", "matched_aliases": ["slavery"], "query_id": "test249", "question_hash": "0b92fb76f5188f189e1b8d3b4cbdfd3bc420c47b4689b5e2731d4c8c5a748d0a", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "ed3a1382ae7cff48db33c4c6612fe5eb8a0cfb778202d4879b7c507e25cc337d", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1015825415, "similarity": 0.01876675603217158, "source_content_hash": "814f1d876e0acface84290f23abaf1b95fe8aa0a30012e5e8b996f566dade4ec", "source_hash": "b247268f4c98e7fcc7e6bda4f2665df9a5a176056c3bdde8df56ed5c9e95593f", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "e7ec96a45c7e151c28b4296a315218905859df5dba2194e26895d23ec2e80a93", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc9185", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "The primary catalyst for secession was slavery, especially Southern political leaders' resistance to attempts by Northern antislavery political forces to block the expansion of slavery into the western territories. Another explanation for secession, and the subsequent formation of the Confederacy, was white Southern nationalism. The primary reason for the North to reject secession was to preserve the Union, a cause based on American nationalism. Most of the debate is about the first question, as to why some southern states decided to secede.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1049958729, "similarity": 0.9945454545454545, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "a37284654c185d7af56fa82005d0246aff6808119e3ef0c417680215af4e6f23", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "slavery", "matched_aliases": ["slavery"], "query_id": "test249", "question_hash": "0b92fb76f5188f189e1b8d3b4cbdfd3bc420c47b4689b5e2731d4c8c5a748d0a", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "11bb2c95d3bbd96bb186584bec2db1168020fd2bf816dff27428159f8f50ec7c", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 16917151, "similarity": 0.0352, "source_content_hash": "0e71793e71c74215c02ef628a013a75ee41b94decc75f00ba82afdcb3f20d442", "source_hash": "cf849f05ea4d49173aa4717f4ee0b00990c6957549abf116a0ec50b8e842c808", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "812fdd9aba167be0d13df9545009d18683b386658389154b9a66311d142896ce", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc9405", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Once water has infiltrated the soil, it remains in the soil, percolates down to the groundwater table, or becomes part of the subsurface runoff process.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 398462219, "similarity": 0.993421052631579, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "9796d0268a6fe7847770c39608b909e0601af02a8a45138361f7cb2fb1e420a7", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "runoff", "matched_aliases": ["runoff"], "query_id": "test251", "question_hash": "fdfc433abea589f071d5229865ca3b79dc10b626a609c3ff58ee8b9362c225b0", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "c1597f7be1ee27d25cb915c2eab4facbd3da8e118d35da40ccf2c79e17e0af06", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 72042227, "similarity": 0.584192439862543, "source_content_hash": "357501a2ab22445c049cceb54c2a9df8d966f54e173e5caa128d441608c45d11", "source_hash": "69eb3c2f60129417b33351ce5325fe89f31b0b304b930fc94286fcb91c13563b", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "306f9765d09d7b7cdfd7cd20f0781a81a00381eb5b6aa47087abdf41d3dfb340", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc9487", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Muhammad Yunus (Bengali: মুহাম্মদ ইউনূস; born 28 June 1940) is a Bangladeshi social entrepreneur, banker, economist, and civil society leader who was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for founding the Grameen Bank and pioneering the concepts of microcredit and microfinance. These loans are given to entrepreneurs who are too poor to qualify for traditional bank loans. In 2006, Yunus and the Grameen Bank were jointly awarded the Nobel Peace Prize \"for their efforts through microcredit to create economic and social development from below\". The Norwegian Nobel Committee stated that \"lasting peace cannot be achieved unless large population groups find ways in which to break out of poverty\" and that \"across cultures and civilizations, Yunus and Grameen Bank have shown that even the poorest of the poor can work to bring about their own development\".[2] Yunus has received several other national and international honours, including the United States Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2009 and the Congressional Gold Medal in 2010.[3]", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1736349413, "similarity": 0.9815354713313897, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "808efaf5064d038e6f5c976602d1e99087ae7ff770a285ad1fbbd477e7437fee", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Muhammad Yunus", "matched_aliases": ["Muhammad Yunus"], "query_id": "test254", "question_hash": "cba7c9b4de0923df6539710e5b96785876931a1a7258f90aec444e687acd395e", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "d10c9100f0e47d7b5adac313ba866933e3ccc1247d848964712b00311697725b", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 35508162, "similarity": 0.44394868934746234, "source_content_hash": "09aeb0e39329f1c7aaa325726c08649404e91ea61dd164c96f6e2779ecaa0072", "source_hash": "5c65e1f1e93e31491aa9f9fca31f584386b187b9cf4d55b5e26ac00fb4431456", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "d71d074089cfd80e7e7446d169ab952c5421c59d68b2cc12345c6fdc81d78de9", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc9497", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": false, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "In 1976, during visits to the poorest households in the village of Jobra near Chittagong University, Muhammad Yunus discovered that very small loans could make a disproportionate difference to a poor person. Village women who made bamboo furniture had to take usurious loans to buy bamboo, and repay their profits to the lenders. Traditional banks did not want to make tiny loans at reasonable interest to the poor due to high risk of default. But Yunus believed that, given the chance, the poor will repay the money and hence microcredit was a viable business model. Yunus lent US$27 of his money to 42 women in the village, who made a profit of BDT 0.50 (US$0.02) each on the loan. Thus, Yunus is credited with the idea of microcredit.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 296303063, "similarity": 0.988458927359131, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "2ca1a04bc982c66e4d0a39c5e0a4194061a2523af1be6c35fafcdc383c1be9c4", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Muhammad Yunus", "matched_aliases": ["Muhammad Yunus"], "query_id": "test254", "question_hash": "cba7c9b4de0923df6539710e5b96785876931a1a7258f90aec444e687acd395e", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "fc33c6eda00aed43f904ed992fbf092e0c455eb604b189de7e51db20cde49ca8", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1048042694, "similarity": 0.16133004926108374, "source_content_hash": "25ef94ced1caadf7945de46ba9bc360803cae010cd1f1ae7089d6147f057db57", "source_hash": "af0594d92af3ebbff0f0f62fb1ee76ce182cb29966956d230fa62a5efc3c00e9", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "80c70f826de6f1bb720835b64ac3c850de64a0e77d5a87609ac26029e9369589", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc9596", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "World Consumer Rights Day is celebrated on 15 March every year. The theme for 2018 is Fairer Digital Marketplaces. Consumers International is calling for: 1. Access to fair and secure internet for the over half the world that are still offline. 2. Action against scams and fraud. 3. Better general consumer protection online.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1979397058, "similarity": 0.9954058192955589, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "c18713eca8240d7df2cee62cad5da9e32b6892f34a59878f7d1aa6905027a8e9", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "15 March every year", "matched_aliases": ["15 March"], "query_id": "test256", "question_hash": "0fd7d35415c0c5f0cdaeaf9f25c2eaf50b45baa6e2204fd46f4f7a3eacb5cc1e", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "c1a97d3f785de59ccb072761de3f65d0b363c81bc3d095b92f54865ae2641a4a", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 727839599, "similarity": 0.14502529510961215, "source_content_hash": "f44636d60a3ef5680a499f9bd37966cbc526d477c2423644af05d2afce6dfa6a", "source_hash": "287769e4728615e7ee7dae6a63365ec2ba39be62c2d0271055a1f39bde7683a9", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "e41b6de2e7a9c901b12e666de315f696c2bc1bc666fbffdf679c3ce451109d99", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc9603", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "A compression release engine brake, frequently called a Jake Nett brake or Jacobs brake, is an engine braking mechanism installed on some diesel engines. When activated, it opens exhaust valves in the cylinders after the compression cycle, releasing the compressed air trapped in the cylinders, and slowing the vehicle.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 463296372, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "af5afcd9fd1c665833d4a08b54a8c7cd3fab9be620fde34c2b758e84d5e6b4a3", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "slowing the vehicle", "matched_aliases": ["slowing the vehicle"], "query_id": "test257", "question_hash": "56f2fce85b22288d160d5500678a5bf352225136d2dc700c70448d8c4df7a02f", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "8155ef84679d343f41b78e4d219093911044fba54a7015cbc62c9f1256224141", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 57120051, "similarity": 0.6075187969924812, "source_content_hash": "128761faa6bde9ecd4f3a9bdbf152a44f2328aa2491816ba3b2103729c05cb03", "source_hash": "5b584613402421c19ada816cf4877c123c0332e4afc5bea93538899fbfe9a484", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "d38c5b85f29ab456521c054733d5a072a5440ed22932e6ef6eea5c6bef755a6f", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc9700", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "The authors of the song have repeatedly rejected this interpretation and have strongly and consistently denied that they intended any references to drug use. Leonard Lipton has stated \"Puff the Magic Dragon is not about drugs.\" Peter Yarrow has frequently explained that the song is about the hardships of growing older and has no relationship to drug-taking. He has also said of the song that it \"never had any meaning other than the obvious one\" and is about the \"loss of innocence in children,\" and dismissed the suggestion of association with drugs as \"sloppy research.\"", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 2053896510, "similarity": 0.9761092150170648, "temperature": 0.3}, {"attempt": 2, "generated_text": "Leonard Lipton and Peter Yarrow have both clarified that the song \"Puff, the Magic Dragon\" does not reference drugs. Peter Yarrow, in particular, has emphasized that the lyrics depict the struggles of aging and the loss of innocence, and he has explicitly dismissed the claim that the song is about drug use as unsound.", "reason": "missing_gold_answer", "seed": 639066374, "similarity": null, "temperature": 0.5}], "fingerprint": "c1e490f1e2c6c8cb8a09494f993417c612bb997a410bc8857f7c2a7e510da783", "generation_attempts": 3, "gold_answer": "the hardships of growing older", "matched_aliases": ["the hardships of growing older"], "query_id": "test263", "question_hash": "734c907826f94c2bb0baa76d3aace3f96c4cad3da01215eb49380413148d3ed7", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "b3b15bc5f7d338b0b2b5b075d9dea9e89832f1eff98ba88f64ceda7a47e0ee6d", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1429494424, "similarity": 0.039627039627039624, "source_content_hash": "41e1abf8b9f914e211f511df471086ce2574a8e1b79a53a96892e60a3b1ea51a", "source_hash": "4e397a7039c43fdc200f43cd9b0c9faba832a44bb077cb06e82373c94d74a