{"doc_id": "doc30463", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "On September 10, 2013, AMC officially cancelled the series after 38 episodes and three seasons. However, on November 15, 2013, Netflix ordered a fourth and final season of six episodes, which was released on Netflix on August 1, 2014.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 75366448, "similarity": 0.957983193277311, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "873d170bd55bcc97e96c0dcc12ac20c68ecdd060b7300d98d1b1be5024745f4a", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "6", "matched_aliases": ["six"], "query_id": "test847", "question_hash": "fbbc10e8e7aa302d6459a6aa30560cd6e0aa691a39df7cbe0bde734ab529cec8", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "9220cd4d20a62443cb282191e318d101119739832445ba8535eb8a48bdb4f2d0", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 325859844, "similarity": 0.5524193548387096, "source_content_hash": "3eefb5ab8d9fa191be8d39dca7ad64bad0a0d5b9b6a67cb9ff715b94f963903a", "source_hash": "2b362d82cd2c0d4d6d7495a994d5fde7a1ae3477dce4f4c93fe7cb9cf01253cc", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "1ba7b74d52fc1e6aa0f5d6121dc452b4ac62a2619bb1c0be01ae4ffa35327c8f", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc30467", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Professor Kantorek gives an impassioned speech about the glory of serving in the Army and \"saving the Fatherland\". On the brink of becoming men, the boys in his class, led by Paul Baumer, are moved to join the army as the new 2nd Company. Their romantic delusions are quickly broken during their brief but rigorous training under the abusive Sergeant Himmelstoss, who bluntly informs them, \"You're going to be soldiersâ€\"and that's all.\"", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 582807765, "similarity": 0.9977116704805492, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "93f7eebcd61f3979f97e2636d98944b9e9e060dd8bc6e02bfbb32c04a62172b3", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Paul Baumer", "matched_aliases": ["Paul Baumer"], "query_id": "test848", "question_hash": "78005f1405979a96a07058dc2b847cb309e17cc1fe376527f1c7b2a4e355d884", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "b754ae32bfd9254246d96b2e2056b5fb9f6f4b04f4197da5f962e4cfaa07c9d9", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 981217775, "similarity": 0.18337408312958436, "source_content_hash": "c9d670ab4f7593a1a294ffbee9d9d85624d418a580055c62d53a749fc8384fa2", "source_hash": "73638562ba171afad30223cce3f6fc624d5455579fa36e1db6828aed5131aa87", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "f851086249d0de02dab0f49a81f5481b6d0ad717de13cd2dc21c2b709ec69c79", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc30640", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "In 1953, the United States Committee for UNICEF, now called the U.S. Fund for UNICEF, started actively promoting the program.[7] By the 1960s, the concept had expanded throughout the United States, with small orange collection boxes distributed to millions of trick-or-treaters.[9] When UNICEF won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1965, U.S. President Lyndon B. Johnson said in his congratulatory letter: \"Your UNICEF Trick or Treat Day has helped turn a holiday too often marred by youthful vandalism into a program of basic training in world citizenship.\"[10] In 1967, Johnson declared Halloween, October 31, to be 'UNICEF Day' in the United States; by 1969, 3.5 million American children were trick-or-treating for donations.[9] Children (and adults) in the U.S. have collected more than US $175 million for Trick-or-Treat for UNICEF.[5][11] Donations to Trick-or-Treat for UNICEF support UNICEF's global programing, but in 2005, half of the proceeds were targeted to a domestic cause, aiding victims of Hurricane Katrina.[12] In 2008, the U.S. Fund for UNICEF introduced mobile phone text message donations as well as a MySpace and Facebook page.[13] In 2014, the U.S. Fund for UNICEF partnered with Crowdrise to expand the campaign's online presence, allowing participants to create personal fundraising web pages in addition to traditional door-to-door trick-or-treating.[14]", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1976128472, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "c804c62cc9baa08086ed82e75dbbdc9b15b2afdd9eb45ef9c7641e7a6d2343b5", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "UNICEF's global programing", "matched_aliases": ["UNICEF's global programing"], "query_id": "test853", "question_hash": "bf348890ae6e176c42cf1f113121a23322f9b4a0a2a8ed2d3dfe9a5e7a9a0828", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "e38c