{"doc_id": "doc41558", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "\"I Wanna Be Sedated\" is a song by the American punk rock band the Ramones. It is one of the band's best known songs.[1] It was originally released on their fourth album, Road to Ruin, in September 1978 and was the B-side of the UK single \"She's the One\" released on September 21,1978.[2] The song was later released as a single in the Netherlands in 1978,[3] then in the U.S. in 1980 by RSO Records from the Times Square soundtrack album.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 381050740, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "54145b9760f04d55f815a24520f178841ad3fe937f0673a6f51be7638af7d11f", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "the Ramones", "matched_aliases": ["the Ramones"], "query_id": "test1172", "question_hash": "20d5d4ee5e473df49ba1c2c5bf1c51239b24bf4d02e73b42b1eb5acfeae6ceed", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "aa57e3211525521f1a842f01450109cb50e2618e76abb38927b471ed9f0f08b5", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 16212071, "similarity": 0.3956639566395664, "source_content_hash": "983bfa351b467460cfe1d950aeeead8ce9a3e6763747992312a012c736cc144f", "source_hash": "78c263483866eb994813bf2173abc131627e90244a4b02cbda587a493772fd35", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "c8c75df2dbe2f002e6d6169820e7fded576c48c336364e6d416fc19d53450626", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc41567", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Zhōngguó is the most common sinitic name for China in modern times. The first appearance of 中國 on an artifact was in the Western Zhou on a ritual vessel known as He zun.[2] It is formed by combining the characters zhōng (中) meaning \"central\" or \"middle\", and guó (國/国), representing \"state\" or \"states\"; in contemporary usage, \"nation\". Prior to the Qin unification of China \"Zhongguo\" referred to the \"Central States\"; the connotation was the primacy of a culturally distinct core area, centered on the Yellow River valley, as distinguished from the tribal periphery.[3] In later periods, however, \"Zhongguo\" was not used in this sense. Dynastic names were used for the state in Imperial China and concepts of the state aside from the ruling dynasty were little understood.[1] Rather, the country was called by the name of the dynasty, such as \"Han dynasty\" (Hanchao 漢朝), \"Tang dynasty\" (Tangchao 唐朝), \"The Great Ming\" (Daming 大明), \"The Great Qing\" (Daqing 大清), as the case might be. Until the 19th century when the international system came to require a common legal language, there was no need for a fixed or unique name.[4]", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 501803839, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "96ef2aacf394128fff490215ed956a651c38364cd46094b375a0236ac41953eb", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Central States", "matched_aliases": ["Central States"], "query_id": "test1173", "question_hash": "4ea1da3a720abf09b353c45e80f4c0b986f2d4535de609f6f6eeb901250968c4", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "0c6aa96a6fde1b7a540c8da912260f272fde9c6f7877250235cd24df7a593901", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1818524615, "similarity": 0.025974025974025976, "source_content_hash": "3ba904974bf9a2941dc60f11110a6dffdae2685e98e5583a7a143e59923e1305", "sour