{"doc_id": "doc46823", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "A standing rib roast, also known as prime rib, is a cut of beef from the primal rib, one of the nine primal cuts of beef. While the entire rib section comprises ribs six through 12, a standing rib roast may contain anywhere from two to seven ribs.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1193674387, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "9951182ff80210a6991efe42066015213d99d062d204ca34a0239909a09a540f", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "the primal rib", "matched_aliases": ["the primal rib"], "query_id": "test1322", "question_hash": "669e99df0441972e7e0d954791807585227874f341f9ea0f64ffbc214e446798", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "7bf7dfd3dd8dd3f517dc2853c71bc401918d1ae72a57f899952712e0b9654c1c", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 568218144, "similarity": 0.2833333333333333, "source_content_hash": "f72343ea4dca6eb139ab758d37fefea87dffe9bdedc74b7b67ea3ffe53f28501", "source_hash": "f5a8d9e036f31b08e7241f0448854265d3944297a8aae9f2c55162f3a863bc18", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "d0089602270edea48c51230cc8a0f9bbf00a0b319309bc5ebc6c87a32cb31114", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc46841", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "One of the most notable effects of the adoption of the Charter was to greatly expand the scope of judicial review, because the Charter is more explicit with respect to the guarantee of rights and the role of judges in enforcing them than was the Bill of Rights. The courts, when confronted with violations of Charter rights, have struck down unconstitutional federal and provincial statutes and regulations or parts of statutes and regulations, as they did when Canadian case law was primarily concerned with resolving issues of federalism. The Charter, however, granted new powers to the courts to enforce remedies that are more creative and to exclude more evidence in trials. These powers are greater than what was typical under the common law and under a system of government that, influenced by Canada's parent country the United Kingdom, was based upon Parliamentary supremacy. As a result, the Charter has attracted both broad support from a majority of the Canadian electorate and criticisms by opponents of increased judicial power. The Charter only applies to government laws and actions (including the laws and actions of federal, provincial, and municipal governments and public school boards), and sometimes to the common law, not to private activity.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 550609550, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}, {"attempt": 2, "generated_text": "One of the most notable effects of the adoption of the Charter was to greatly expand the scope of judicial review, because the Charter is more explicit with respect to the guarantee of rights and the role of judges in enforcing them than was the Bill of Rights. The courts, when confronted with violations of Charter rights, have struck down unconstitutional federal and provincial statutes and regulations or parts of statutes and regulations, as they did when Canadian case law was primarily concerned with resolving issues of federalism. The Charter, however, granted new powers to the courts to enforce remedies that are more creative and to exclude more evidence in trials. These powers are greater than what was typical under the common law and under a system of government that, influenced by Canada's parent country the United Kingdom, was based upon Parliamentary supremacy. As a result, the Charter has attracted both broad support from a majority of the Canadian electorate and criticisms by opponents of increased judicial power. The Charter only applies to government laws and actions (including the laws and actions of federal, provincial, and municipal governments and public school boards), and sometimes to the common law, not to private activity.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1661221072, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.5}], "fingerprint": "32cb8fa8af8347c83aecffe738e4c9879ffd9247e45a14bffb8f3ef43a204c73", "generation_attempts": 3, "gold_answer": "Supreme Court of Canada", "matched_aliases": ["the courts"], "query_id": "test1324", "question_hash": "7e25d5f689cdc42f31a448d494c2f1d51d42d06a5339a1aa9d30afbf3e022c4b", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "aba280de283b5df00ca7d0c2278e125644f25b10bc37eb97acf572ec56367a91", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 2067139673, "similarity": 0.05716554188169909, "source_content_hash": "f73a93e5018f51de48e18d8b0e6c66766380c74847996fc28dfef27b0b76de19", "source_hash": "ea5d85d5c6cacdb32553cbc38e6e8110e254e0bd17d02e8b6cd2487baac77c79", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.7, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "b096e8edc96f66b9e3316f979efa7d142ca3fef1da2f4fd1ad26f5dd61bf5328", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc46842", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Under the Charter, people physically present in Canada have numerous civil and political rights. Most of the rights can be exercised by any legal person (the Charter does not define the corporation as a \"legal person\"),[3] but a few of the rights belong exclusively to natural persons, or (as in sections 3 and 6) only to citizens of Canada. The rights are enforceable by the courts through section 24 of the Charter, which allows courts discretion to award remedies to those whose rights have been denied. This section also allows courts to exclude evidence in trials if the evidence was acquired in a way that conflicts with the Charter and might damage the reputation of the justice system. Section 32 confirms that the Charter is binding on the federal government, the territories under its authority, and the provincial governments. The rights and freedoms enshrined in 34 sections of the Charter include:", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 234461678, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "6c0cabc2289b95815d7ac2ec03a85399859b70ec5064bdc9c965904cff31330e", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Supreme Court of Canada", "matched_aliases": ["the courts"], "query_id": "test1324", "question_hash": "7e25d5f689cdc42f31a448d494c2f1d51d42d06a5339a1aa9d30afbf3e022c4b", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "844d4a0b1d3d6df8831c5fce483ea0c442f4abd36f356c3775128898d1f3d860", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1495680241, "similarity": 0.27932203389830507, "source_content_hash": "34060ebfd535b5424540f942a1b515b5765b6727cab9a4ceed298f0b9d0d42d7", "source_hash": "b3ef32accbe80e19d5fd7b28ca78cad2b1edc9e6d3eb7e2364545a660237bec6", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "5fd8ae3beb201481ee07280b7184b7df8e8374e303bf2af3