{"doc_id": "doc56674", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "\"You'll Never Find Another Love Like Mine\" (written by Kenny Gamble & Leon Huff) is a song performed by R&B singer Lou Rawls on his 1976 album All Things in Time. The song proved to be Rawls' breakthrough hit, reaching number one on both the R&B[1] and Easy Listening charts as well as number four on the dance chart [2] and number two on the US Billboard Hot 100, where it was kept from the top spot for two weeks by \"You Should Be Dancing\" by The Bee Gees[3] and \"(Shake, Shake, Shake) Shake Your Booty\" by KC and the Sunshine Band,[4] respectively. This was the first and only time that one of Rawls' records managed to reach Billboard's pop top ten. It was the first big hit for Philadelphia International to feature the reformulated MFSB, after many of the original members left Gamble and Huff for better opportunities.[5] The song started Rawls' live shows from 1977 on.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1961313071, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "d609b103ecea10ee392a1ff06a96155e62f5282ae134a6429b573f50c3ee2f0d", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Lou Rawls", "matched_aliases": ["Lou Rawls"], "query_id": "test1595", "question_hash": "2fa689f5b14f1b88b47203abbb460717c74d904d200a02290a7b04124be05dfc", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "03308b719e780a8e81f4e7dc4a4d00f6cee33b8472517e2787bad2e400412bee", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 856635318, "similarity": 0.4878640776699029, "source_content_hash": "87d43b07bf87b01db39027009f25d0c1884c03e0164ceadad4f0df2891276c8d", "source_hash": "8a9fba018b92d52be9264aba164a456d9b772bdf3635c8bd716ef05025296a86", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "130a0cf98ae21feebb2c660eb200f163c64ce00a1d40a66ca9947a30006cb52e", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc56817", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Philippe Petit (French pronunciation: [filip pəti]; born 13 August 1949) is a French high-wire artist who gained fame for his high-wire walk between the Twin Towers of the World Trade Center in New York City, on the morning of August 7, 1974 as well as his high wire walk between the Notre Dame cathedral in Paris, 1971. For his unauthorized feat 400 metres (1,000 feet) above the ground – which he referred to as \"le coup\" – he rigged a 200-kilogram (440-pound) cable and used a custom-made 8-metre (30-foot) long, 25-kilogram (55-pound) balancing pole. He performed for 45 minutes, making eight passes along the wire. The following week, he celebrated his 25th birthday. All charges were dismissed in exchange for him doing a performance in Central Park for children.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1272284916, "similarity": 0.9941747572815534, "temperature": 0.3}, {"attempt": 2, "generated_text": "Philippe Petit (French pronunciation: [filip pəti]; born 13 August 1949) is a French high-wire artist who gained fame for his high-wire walk between the Twin Towers of the World Trade Center in New York City, on the morning of August 7, 1974, as well as his high-wire walk between the Notre Dame cathedral in Paris in 1971. For his unauthorized feat of 400 metres (1,000 feet) above the ground – which he referred to as \"le coup\" – he rigged a 200-kilogram (440-pound) cable and used a custom-made 8-metre (30-foot) long, 25-kilogram (55-pound) balancing pole. He performed for 45 minutes, making eight passes along the wire. The following week, he celebrated his 25th birthday. All charges were dismissed in exchange for him doing a performance in Central Park for children.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1447752721, "similarity": 0.961960025789813, "temperature": 0.5}], "fingerprint": "f0d9cc87b490cd221ad8687f269fa119d1770bfef746dec465f279552b74b2c3", "generation_attempts": 3, "gold_answer": "Philippe Petit", "matched_aliases": ["Philippe Petit"], "query_id": "test1602", "question_hash": "2ad949463a4fac84e0fd187731a7998f34dff2ad7c7ff4162cb765c4206a55ec", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "44d000fd9381edf04c031f9d5a47549c561e83f8f448da1cb051e64986ff0476", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1694497347, "similarity": 0.1620795107033639, "source_content_hash": "b513b546016a6b8a529257093d7bafb2efb6dc73b91854b46294515bbc34db2e", "source_hash": "26b191f7bc8b445872a156d296b26814d189fbc242901f9fc523125ed569e2b8", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.7, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "10933a8d58dfba5a94db37319cb872daaa06777f764cad3c59102efcf5b65d6e", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc56894", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "During the 1995–96 season, Alan Shearer became the first player to score 100 Premier League goals, and holds the record for the fewest games taken to reach 100, doing so in 124 appearances. He also holds the record for most goals scored in the Premier League. After Shearer, Harry Kane is the second-fastest to 100 goals, doing so in 141 games.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1461228168, "similarity": 0.9870875179340028, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "57292e705082264a4004c2de66ab3afa5c30d788fc0b37b72f2e654ddd7238b4", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Alan Shearer", "matched_aliases": ["Alan Shearer"], "query_id": "test1606", "question_hash": "8ea3d6b7a5bcb811c1f556081fd78ca255a40ce970b6c437fc429be01f34a104", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "418ef72eef570eb858606186f25d4f915eb7734d5fb63ef7b0aafdaf9fa8d4c9", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1008686670, "similarity": 0.91005291005291, "source_content_hash": "cb72ec9b56de346cee34dc98b75a139fa70139e80cf4f455940269d8bfc64099", "source_hash": "48021ceea4344f01c2c944484c01a0155b9e1499baaeaebc58af5a9e553a1668", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "64b2a2969c47696726930e82a71ecdcb9852818f5f08cfad215e2734da48a967", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc56897", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "The Pakistani National Assembly (Urdu: قومی اسمبلئ پاکستان‬‎— Qaumī Asimbli'e Pākistān); is the lower house of the bicameral Majlis-e-Shura, which also comprises the President of Pakistan and Senate (upper house). The National Assembly and the Senate both convene at Parliament House in Islamabad. The National Assembly is a democratically elected body consisting of a total of 332 members who are referred to as Members of the National Assembly (MNAs), of which 272 are directly elected members and 70 reserved seats for women and religious minorities. A political party must secure 172 seats to obtain and preserve a majority.[3]", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1989714735, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "03b630ee2a0eaf9d456ad6d159aa279d1e7d995a85417e643f8b29ff5f64e449", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "332 members", "matched_aliases": ["332 members", "332"], "query_id": "test1607", "question_hash": "e0f4729ad6e1754bf4ad75bafd6c65ca50903ccb189800bb00d3195bda6582ac", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "1016ea1f6c605380c227bd960d18f89e13ea4a546b17956482598c6107ede8cd", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 551305320, "similarity": 0.614100185528757, "source_content_hash": "a0d6d37fa34e8ed74c02daee6f7737e14eb1c5ab0afdd4270adee2d2292b9772", "source_hash": "ad51e6996fed47b52d379095286ab399088022d5acf6b6da00402e045f19fac6", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "12b06c0f510359031e44860d52574d4c95791b481a3365a294408139338cfd9b", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash"