{"doc_id": "doc68766", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "The first modern usage of the specific term \"social justice\" is typically attributed to Catholic thinkers from the 1840s, including the Jesuit Luigi Taparelli in *Civiltà Cattolica*, based on the work of St. Thomas Aquinas. He argued that rival capitalist and socialist theories, based on subjective Cartesian thinking, undermined the unity of society present in Thomistic metaphysics as neither were sufficiently concerned with moral philosophy. Writing in 1861, the influential British philosopher and economist, John Stuart Mill stated in *Utilitarianism* his view that \"Society should treat all equally well who have deserved equally well of it, that is, who have deserved equally well absolutely. This is the highest abstract standard of social and distributive justice; towards which all institutions, and the efforts of all virtuous citizens, should be made in the utmost degree to converge.\"", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 345056229, "similarity": 0.9799777530589544, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "6f90192653df58c74b859456eea151025c34459c1932b93d488cd5ecc3dd0378", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "the 1840s", "matched_aliases": ["the 1840s"], "query_id": "test1958", "question_hash": "70be117ab9c3f61b81e34c35d2f59fd98945582dc3c94aa06804285e22205a8c", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "3f5e515b59bf5c9e5bfff3f74c54e76bd5cc2ed7a7f68b3c04e0cb742f2227d4", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 287193888, "similarity": 0.25203252032520324, "source_content_hash": "db7a5710b383e54c3013f901f7d5d8627c46b5323639143c5ed8b9b5891de2b2", "source_hash": "e6edc57499df3ebf96f07935bac244f9b36bd5d898aec6ce4c8063d87999158a", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "2610fc5259f4ab5d79392a6df1c36fe968dd5f2ba71f7a4a445d4997f9