{"doc_id": "doc69946", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Saint Alphonsa, F.C.C., (born Anna Muttathupadathu; 19 August 1910 â€\" 28 July 1946) was an Indian religious sister and educator. She was the first woman of Indian origin to be canonised as a saint by the Catholic Church, and the first canonised saint of the Syro-Malabar Catholic Church, an Eastern Catholic Church based in India. Her feast day is observed on July 28th.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 2036192084, "similarity": 0.9973045822102425, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "0e459500d5e382acfdbf1e5709cde6571d5f14f89f3005d793ec4228483dca7b", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "Saint Alphonsa", "matched_aliases": ["Saint Alphonsa"], "query_id": "test1995", "question_hash": "57c4011c44d9a2a6470c42a8001e98da4585d932ee06c7564619a9e18cbdfae1", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "368b3e5fa70deef1df1fa4278f961ea0092ed745ae4f50d81313b060719331f1", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1543169445, "similarity": 0.5333333333333333, "source_content_hash": "e6c54d6ca3a40b30cf1a36ef6a706198119a26381a4b71007d26a0f9dd7974d8", "source_hash": "869ced4bd866ec4c0deb7b6bad8424ad2e1967bef42e2d035e42946ef079270a", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "b48da6dadf93119f1575cdbe32162dcb950bef6c8b47b7834947da1e6b69737d", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc69978", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "However, reference to the harp as the arms of Ireland can be found in one of the oldest medieval rolls of arms. The Wijnbergen Roll, a French roll of arms dating from c. 1280 and preserved in The Hague, Netherlands, attributed \"D'azure a la harpe d'or\" (English: Blue with a harp of gold) to the King of Ireland (\"le Roi d'Irlande\").[3][4] The harp, traditionally associated with King David, was a rare charge on medieval rolls and only two arms with a harp are listed in a collection of 19 early rolls.[5] Triangular devices appeared on medieval Irish coinage by kings John and Edward I in the 12th and 13th centuries. These devices may have been crude harps or it may be that the harp developed from the use of triangles to distinguish Irish coins.[6] The idea of a harp being the arms of Ireland may have originated as a reference to a fictional character, le roi d'irelande, in the courtly legend cycle of Tristan. Alternatively, it may have derived from a celebrated 13th century bardic poem, Tabhroidh Chugam Cruit mo Riogh, dedicated to the Donnchadh Cairbreach O'Briain (d. 1242), a Gaelic King of Thomond.[7]", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 965685863, "similarity": 0.9946571682991986, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "d48bfd43ce86b87dfe48f3dee750dcb24aeaa29c2faf6a80b8238e94f40cc980", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "the arms of Ireland", "matched_aliases": ["the arms of Ireland"], "query_id": "test1996", "question_hash": "fa970d07cf70da6dcc7563a9de7139af0fb3d87b9363a4e521ed90d82a223f55", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "7dc754742eb7f7188e51a5af5aa1a79a94b48eaf5b2878d51bbf2c5b0a1d05a9", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 798327384, "similarity": 0.19914346895074947, "source_content_hash": "5fce372748b86676fda5df936cbd58a12a45cbd088b83848ec7916d9c1d2a8f1", "source_hash