{"doc_id": "doc87611", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Autumn, also known as fall in American and Canadian English, is one of the four temperate seasons. Autumn marks the transition from summer to winter, in September (Northern Hemisphere) or March (Southern Hemisphere), when the duration of daylight becomes noticeably shorter and the temperature cools down considerably. One of its main features is the shedding of leaves from deciduous trees.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 131674675, "similarity": 0.9961783439490446, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "3b20a5dd0972b01bdf2487b6533b0fa1f80ea7e76f31ef8821da10d0cc28f9cf", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "fall", "matched_aliases": ["fall"], "query_id": "test2548", "question_hash": "398e4cc71b82f256582166adffac8a068f46426d63102bff47933b4d82a10a56", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "12a838d58e2b307c6c15e9ab3044fc5bf82911471333f504e5b2caf479fc48af", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1100925499, "similarity": 0.026737967914438502, "source_content_hash": "7b549f773e9e068e34fa5aea3e5bf3729349118b7d5a365cacb6c421e90ef62c", "source_hash": "7dc32c0a3a5f389827310a6b7f768acaf005b9d486dabd6de5d9d43e94421eb7", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "b85af0f461e8c2805e2afe2bfd7c44b5121ad3b8d0f4acd9cfd7645e30a2a119", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc87615", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Before the 16th century, harvest was the term usually used to refer to the season, as it is common in other West Germanic languages to this day (cf. Dutch herfst, German Herbst and Scots hairst). However, as more people gradually moved from working the land to living in towns, the word harvest lost its reference to the time of year and came to refer only to the actual activity of reaping, and autumn, as well as fall, began to replace it as a reference to the season.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1088985219, "similarity": 0.9915611814345991, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "9b0fc0a16a37b59253c4a262b25a0e1b947504f1a6caab8c2cb178f8782797fa", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "fall", "matched_aliases": ["fall"], "query_id": "test2548", "question_hash": "398e4cc71b82f256582166adffac8a068f46426d63102bff47933b4d82a10a56", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "dab14f7ead7aeafc53d087c4aba64c56d5cbeee7bd317f64a73884e69f787509", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1454647100, "similarity": 0.10400866738894908, "source_content_hash": "42736aaa9e9088388ce70c552c685901b9f44dc0c9821813278e20b103e08f16", "source_hash": "b441877e1a89b2e5dc7f395230d012a01603b1220b2da1afd2a24048eceabdb1", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "509955bbf3a031f5feae10a2bd2de3fdd6fbac54c02499f5a4ba4ffd53d36b20", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc87812", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Fayetteville has received the All-America City Award from the National Civic League three times. As of the 2010 census it had a population of 200,564,[5] with an estimated population of 204,408 in 2013.[6] It is the 6th-largest city in North Carolina. Fayetteville is in the Sandhills in the western part of the Coastal Plain region, on the Cape Fear River.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1668810140, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}, {"attempt": 2, "generated_text": "Fayetteville is a city in the Sandhills of North Carolina's Coastal Plain region, situated on the Cape Fear River. As of the 2010 census, its population was 200,564, though the 2013 estimate reached 204,408.", "reason": "missing_gold_answer", "seed": 1370278493, "similarity": null, "temperature": 0.5}], "fingerprint": "aad5a7175a91af479676c44fcb62f2881aa0d51973046ada653401540a9b4f02", "generation_attempts": 3, "gold_answer": "204,408 in 2013", "matched_aliases": ["204,408 in 2013"], "query_id": "test2554", "question_hash": "74409af41e03611bda3f897530605b699452253a3eddba2690b8a8b8a555fb02", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "68f3f98afe07c12245621733741bc44ce868c3d4e31ce6921e235b99382ebac1", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 230486834, "similarity": 0.6757215619694398, "source_content_hash": "a97a7962fb0544d7a96e787290a2098a1db7d619e86d6fda9bdd2a55b5b2a6e2", "source_hash": "6f3162c025b0f86035d9984425995558dd2fc5edd62d1fa39539d4d4947bdb8f", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.7, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "0419ea6c044abc84a068c9d43e1325b436d865542b42d8d4ba77cf77a6e8145a", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc87917", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "\"Mercy Mercy Me (The Ecology)\" was the second single from Marvin Gaye's 1971 album, What's Going On. Following the breakthrough of the title track's success, the song, written solely by Gaye, became regarded as one of popular music's most poignant anthems of sorrow regarding the environment. Led by Gaye playing piano, strings conducted by Paul Riser and David Van De Pitte, multi-tracking vocals from Gaye and The Andantes, multiple background instruments provided by The Funk Brothers and a leading sax solo by Wild Bill Moore, the song rose to number 4 on Billboard's Pop Singles chart and number one for two weeks on the R&B Singles charts on August 14 through to August 27, 1971.[1] The distinctive percussive sound heard on the track was a wood block struck by a rubber mallet, drenched in studio reverb.[2] The song also brought Gaye one of his rare appearances on the Adult Contemporary chart, where it peaked at number 34. In Canada, \"Mercy Mercy Me\" spent two weeks at number 9.[3]", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1159392030, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "3366609d536da7a49ef30eb06373ffde70b193d62e75ef1269dbab66bb580041", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "sorrow regarding the environment", "matched_aliases": ["sorrow regarding the environment", "the environment"], "query_id": "test2559", "question_hash": "80126b0cdbaa646432fcd680ac53422ec9900cce71f41108a8e6093768b39d91", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "0a281d174641db811123cbfac13004a80f0f3364deec6c794754f1f659a3a27d", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1226394627, "similarity": 0.7607125662012518, "source_content_hash": "bf7f2de4641c219654c58455ab43d8a529ee73ffcb956369947593391e787160", "source_hash": "8ea415d625f9473aef775b8741678f50baf44a1cfa2318824d53315f6582b425", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "dcc070ba8e4663ad6bc5cba952bce61797cfc345825b11ce69311dd7a60b768e", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
