{"doc_id": "doc99698", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "The occupation of the Baltic states was the military occupation of the three Baltic states—Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania—by the Soviet Union under the auspices of the Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact on 14 June 1940, followed by their incorporation into the USSR as constituent republics, unrecognised by most Western powers. On 22 June 1941, Nazi Germany attacked the USSR and within weeks occupied the Baltic territories. In July 1941, the Baltic territory was incorporated into the Reichskommissariat Ostland of the Third Reich. As a result of the Baltic Offensive of 1944, the Soviet Union recaptured most of the Baltic states and trapped the remaining German forces in the Courland pocket until their formal surrender in May 1945. The Soviet \"annexation occupation\" (Annexionsbesetzung or occupation sui generis) of the Baltic states lasted until August 1991, when the Baltic states regained independence.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 63180284, "similarity": 0.9895547003848268, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "dec67e8d976353a4c148fa4fdeec38b9bfa49fe3257409ed46b02c0d1f50f5b8", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "14 June 1940", "matched_aliases": ["14 June 1940"], "query_id": "test2918", "question_hash": "b9a5d3a2918f8221a2ee7b4b55ad2b5e612f6bef71a05c2065251f77d49c6371", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "d40c0510030f19dfa94239f033e208ead61593f6475af90d4bbcfc2bb6b2d3d2", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 56061748, "similarity": 0.1548480463096961, "source_content_hash": "fadba7c58c74e55af56e28f376ea20cc2a6e4bdaeb37a696c8e10ad08d0cfca2", "source_hash": "f177e1198678ba58862e543ada969ce0f518a6d2566518b5dbff1a1dbddcd87b", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "e99211142d9b0188b07a0170c9e39ed170bd68a3e1b3d0beaeeff120250e0549", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc99789", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "In contrast to separationism, the Supreme Court of the United States in Zorach v. Clauson upheld accommodationism, holding that the nation's \"institutions presuppose a Supreme Being\" and that government recognition of God does not constitute the establishment of a state church as the Constitution's authors intended to prohibit.[3][4] As such, the Court has not always interpreted the constitutional principle as absolute, and the proper extent of separation between government and religion in the U.S. remains an ongoing subject of impassioned debate.[5][6][7][8]", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1875316893, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "2af204aeb23185b982e62d86c81e061c6d6adae8a3f4c55cec21e691775720e3", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "separationism", "matched_aliases": ["separationism"], "query_id": "test2920", "question_hash": "108d59863a9ff5fd2ff9cb849cf4a80b686f2fad990a35b91baf65419be95fa4", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "42852780feab4f0da0ef6e3e520fa60a1b74b585d1eee4409e9a1a5f0512d35b", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 864389001, "similarity": 0.1037037037037037, "source_content_hash": "86de2f5f733c2545c443a9b1cdff2ef8dd2317cd4323443c442351b2c3e18dbf", "source_hash": "789467371acda082179523eb2e2d804eea0c3e14d55e809f65a649b0891e6698", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "ee19436abaa58eb91ee63d55987db9618a88f7649b0cbb8fbe0b0199e8216a77", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc99888", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Flair triumphantly returned to WCW as a hero in February 1993, as a result of a \"no-compete\" clause he was unable to wrestle, so he hosted a short-lived talk show in WCW called A Flair for the Gold. Arn Anderson usually appeared at the bar on the show's set, and Flair's maid Fifi cleaned or bore gifts. Once he returned to action, Flair briefly held the NWA World Heavyweight Championship for a tenth time after defeating Barry Windham at Beach Blast before WCW finally left the NWA in September 1993. At Fall Brawl, Flair lost the title, now rebranded the WCW International World Heavyweight Championship, to \"Ravishing Rick\" Rude. At Starrcade in 1993, Flair defeated Vader to win the WCW World Heavyweight title for the second time. In the spring of 1994, Flair began a tweener turn and started another feud with longtime rival Ricky Steamboat and challenged Steamboat to a match at Spring Stampede which ended in a no contest from a double pin, causing the title to be held up. Flair then defeated Steamboat in a rematch to reclaim the held-up title on an episode of WCW Saturday Night. The WWE does not count this victory as a new title win. Flair then challenged Col. Robert Parker to wrestle one of his men at Slamboree, which turned out to be Barry Windham, whom Flair defeated, afterwards he quietly turned heel and took Sherri Martel as his manager. He would also wrestle Lord Steven Regal in a five-match series under Marquess of Queensberry Rules, which aired on WCW Worldwide between April 30 and May 28, in which Flair won the series, with 2 wins, 1 loss, and 2 draws.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1083648322, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}], "fingerprint": "1e3bf7ec81b95001555bbfb188e21786d68be53cb38558d07b0919deb6eb61c0", "generation_attempts": 2, "gold_answer": "February 1993", "matched_aliases": ["February 1993"], "query_id": "test2921", "question_hash": "1eff6f0e54d7c8ea783e254a69851da50617d630b90533c15a1dbb2b0850da67", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "3de12e07051d9cb1d2ceaa8614564ed5c8d019a06fc2a4b50d0ec6c5c677f91b", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 1798384538, "similarity": 0.19283065512978986, "source_content_hash": "f80eb44b66831128850a8bed04f6566b9b6f1bb95a327907962450cfe9bcef75", "source_hash": "e13fa337983545af713f1505e3bafd7fbbc9f7b17f7fba1a4801b8ae9202b404", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.5, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "6445e3525634720ae50457593229ec008221e5c58a2f2655b6d3b3a6c4ebb8b6", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": true, "not_near_duplicate": true}, "mode": "deterministic", "model": null, "provider": null, "semantic_verified": false, "stage": "correct_generation", "validation_goal": "query_answerability", "validator_hash": "a02bd2beee5a12b1acaa721f9e5fc6aa8fe87d0fc104e403231550d93996298f", "version": "v2"}, "validation_goal": "query_answerability"}}
{"doc_id": "doc99954", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Actor Jason Lee also portrays David Seville in a series of films about Alvin and the Chipmunks which uses a combination of live-action acting and computer animation. While Ross Bagdasarian, Jr. does not do any voices for the film series, the films are all produced in association with Bagdasarian Productions, which owns the rights to all of the characters.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 1188006897, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.3}, {"attempt": 2, "generated_text": "Actor Jason Lee also portrays David Seville in a series of films about Alvin and the Chipmunks which uses a combination of live-action acting and computer animation. While Ross Bagdasarian, Jr. does not do any voices for the film series, the films are all produced in association with Bagdasarian Productions, which owns the rights to all of the characters.", "reason": "near_duplicate_of_source", "seed": 906972202, "similarity": 1.0, "temperature": 0.5}], "fingerprint": "e37cc74bfc9dff647793da701977bb1b05dcc9a752f1e10a7caa1fb0a51467cc", "generation_attempts": 3, "gold_answer": "Jason Lee", "matched_aliases": ["Jason Lee"], "query_id": "test2922", "question_hash": "187ffb46682ff38bc490808597640246090caff014916ef912cf0c49492a7841", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "row_hash": "26ab6122b5f565521c13ffd19fb472c286469c07b91aa37cb25a7d044974f3d1", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 563554532, "similarity": 0.08602150537634409, "source_content_hash": "bf412ba753c1daafca317ef9927b64c19c15f72c726c57b60b5326d11b527b9e", "source_hash": "d9cfde26538f646ccbcd52cd0a5633e881de37d3d3558204a3eb8215562a2a89", "target_answer": null, "temperature": 0.7, "validation": {"answerability_verified": false, "evidence_hash": "9f36e5b25ece3947711f9850b6459e47e0f5321637601960c710ea41cd547c9d", "format_attempts": [], "judgment": {"gold_alias_present": tr